\subsection{\code{BaseRMosaicLayerProcessor$extract_data()}}{
  Read the table out of the recorded \code{mosaicplot()} call.

The emitted shape is the segmented one the stacked bar processor
already produces -- \code{data[[fill]][[category]]} -- because the core
builds \code{MosaicTrace} on \code{SegmentedTrace} and navigates it
category-then-series exactly as a stack. \code{mosaicplot()} splits along
the first dimension into columns and each column along the second, so
the first dimension is the category and the second the fill.

Each cell carries four numbers rather than one:
\itemize{
\item \code{y}, the cell's conditional proportion within its column -- the
tile's height, and what a stack's value would be;
\item \code{width}, the column's share of all observations -- carried on every
cell of the column, because the grammar's unit is the point and a
flat list has nowhere else to put it;
\item \code{count}, the cell's own count. A mosaic is drawn \emph{from} counts and
they are the numbers a reader would quote back;
\item \code{z}, the fill level's name.
}

A column that observed nothing has no conditional proportions to
report -- dividing would give \code{NaN} for every cell -- so its cells
carry a proportion of 0 alongside their true count of 0. That is what
the chart draws: a column of zero width and no tiles.
  \subsection{Usage}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="r">}}
    \preformatted{BaseRMosaicLayerProcessor$extract_data(layer_info)}
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Arguments}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="arguments">}}
    \describe{
      \item{\code{layer_info}}{Information about the recorded plot call}
    }
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Returns}{
    Nested list of points, empty when there is no table to read
  }
}

