\subsection{\code{BaseRMosaicLayerProcessor$extract_axis_titles()}}{
  Name the axes from the table's own dimension names.

\code{mosaicplot()} labels its axes with \code{names(dimnames(x))} unless the
author overrides them -- "Hair" and "Eye" for \code{HairEyeColor[, , 1]} --
so those are the two words a reader should be given for the two
dimensions.

Which grammar axis each lands on is not the chart's own arrangement.
The second dimension is what \code{mosaicplot()} draws up the y axis, but a
segmented layer's \code{y} holds the \emph{magnitude} and its \code{z} the fill, so
the second dimension is named on \code{z} and \code{y} says what its numbers
are. \code{ylab} follows the dimension it names rather than the axis it
shares a letter with, for the same reason.
