\subsection{\code{BaseRMosaicLayerProcessor$generate_selectors()}}{
  Address the tiles the chart drew.

Measured on a rendered \code{mosaicplot(HairEyeColor[, , 1])}: gridGraphics
draws one \code{-polygon-N} grob per cell and nothing else as a polygon --
16 grobs for a 4x4 table, with no frame among them. Their geometry
says the order: \code{polygon-1} to \code{-4} share the leftmost column's x
extent, \code{-5} to \code{-8} the next one's, and within a column the grob
number runs down the fill levels in the table's own order. So the tile
for row \code{f} of column \code{c} is the \code{(c - 1) * fills + f}th grob.

The grobs are \emph{found} rather than named by formula, and a short list
is declined: a guessed id resolves to nothing at best and to another
panel's tiles at worst, and a mosaic with no highlight still reads --
the outcome #145 established for a layer with nothing to point at.
  \subsection{Usage}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="r">}}
    \preformatted{BaseRMosaicLayerProcessor$generate_selectors(layer_info, gt = NULL)}
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Arguments}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="arguments">}}
    \describe{
      \item{\code{layer_info}}{Information about the recorded plot call}
      \item{\code{gt}}{Gtable object to search}
    }
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Returns}{
    List of selectors, one per cell, or empty when unresolvable
  }
}

